WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.120
Hi, I'm Christa Brelsford.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.340
I'm a Liane Russell Fellow at Oak Ridge National
Lab.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:13.190
I've always had an idealistic bent and I can't
think of a better way to spend my career than

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:17.650
trying to understand, prevent, and mitigate
what I see as the biggest risk to our global

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.340
society: climate change.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:25.380
I grew up in rural Alaska in a small village
of 83 people, not far from the villages that

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:30.670
are producing America's first climate refugees
as climate change-driven coastal erosion literally

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:33.230
wipes their villages off the map.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:37.280
I then spent a decade living here in the desert
Southwest where allocation institutions that

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.069
were appropriate for last century's problems
are now creating a situation where one more

00:00:42.069 --> 00:00:46.649
drought might cause major social and economic
upheaval.

00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:51.360
These are complicated hydrological and environmental
systems, but the problems we face because

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.799
of changes in these physical systems will
only be solved through interventions in human

00:00:55.799 --> 00:00:59.789
social structures: our laws, policies, and
behaviors.

00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:04.879
We cannot understand the complex and interdependent
world that we live in without developing theoretical

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:08.789
and empirical models that acknowledge the
role of human social structures, choices,

00:01:08.789 --> 00:01:09.469
and behaviors.

